-- title:
--   Transfinite assembly of complementary prototype pairs along the continuum
-- statement:
--   Notation follows the prototype definitions: $D$ is the countable set of pairs $(m,g)$ with $m\in\mathbb N$ and $g:\{0,1\}^m\to\{0,1\}$ a Boolean function of $m$ variables, $E_0=D\times\{0,1\}$, a prototype is a pair $(T,s)$ with $T\subseteq E_0$ and $s\in\{0,1\}$, $\mathcal B(P)$ is the family of prototype bases of a set $P$ of prototypes, $\pi(B)=\{(B,0),(E_0\setminus B,1)\}$ is the complementary pair generated by $B\subseteq E_0$, and *good* refers to a fixed rank model $R$ on $D$; the stage lemma and the assembly below assume that the ideal of $R$ is the column ideal $\mathcal K$ of the construction. Let $\mathfrak c$ denote the initial ordinal of the continuum, viewed as a well-ordered index set in which every index has fewer than $2^{\aleph_0}$ predecessors. A *schedule* is a map $f$ assigning to each $a\in\mathfrak c$ either nothing (an empty stage) or a set $f(a)=B\subseteq E_0$.
--
--   1. **Stage prototypes.** $\sigma(\text{nothing})=\emptyset$ and $\sigma(B)=\pi(B)$; each $\sigma(\cdot)$ is finite.
--   2. **Past, initial and total families.** For a schedule $f$ and $a\in\mathfrak c$,
--   $$\mathrm{Past}_f(a)=\bigcup_{b<a}\sigma(f(b)),\qquad \mathrm{Init}_f(a)=\mathrm{Past}_f(a)\cup\sigma(f(a)),\qquad \mathrm{All}_f=\bigcup_{a\in\mathfrak c}\sigma(f(a)).$$
--   3. **One stage.** (Lemma.) If $P$ is good and has cardinality smaller than $2^{\aleph_0}$, then for any $I,Y\subseteq E_0$ there is a stage value $o$ (nothing or a set) such that $P\cup\sigma(o)$ is good and, provided $I\subseteq Y$, the family $\mathcal B(P\cup\sigma(o))$ meets $(I,Y)$, i.e. contains some $B$ with $B\subseteq I$, or $I\subseteq B\subseteq Y$, or $Y\subseteq B$.
--   4. **Assembly step.** Fix a map $e:\mathfrak c\to\mathcal P(E_0)\times\mathcal P(E_0)$, $e(a)=(I_a,Y_a)$ (in the application, an enumeration of pairs of subsets of $E_0$). Given values $f(b)$ for $b<a$, let $P=\bigcup_{b<a}\sigma(f(b))$. If $P$ is good, the step chooses a stage value $o$ as in item 3 for the pair $(I_a,Y_a)$ (the cardinality hypothesis holds because $a$ has fewer than $2^{\aleph_0}$ predecessors and every stage is finite); if $P$ is not good, the step returns nothing.
--   5. **Assembled schedule.** $f_e$ is the schedule defined by well-founded recursion on $\mathfrak c$: $f_e(a)$ is the assembly step applied at $a$ to the earlier values $f_e(b)$, $b<a$.
--
--   This is the continuum-length recursion of the construction: when $e$ enumerates all pairs $(I,Y)$ of subsets of $E_0$ along $\mathfrak c$, one pair being handled per stage, the assembled schedule yields a good family $\mathrm{All}_{f_e}$ of prototypes whose prototype bases meet every pair $I\subseteq Y$, and these prototype bases form the basis system of the self-dual matroid on $E_0$.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The index type `CIndex` is `Cardinal.continuum.ord.ToType`; schedules are maps `CIndex → Option (Set E0)` with `none` for an empty stage. The definitions are `stagePrototypes`, `pastPrototypes`, `initialPrototypes`, `allPrototypes`, `RankModel.assemblyStep` (which picks its value with `Classical.choose` from the lemma `RankModel.exists_stage`) and `RankModel.assembled` (via `WellFounded.fix`); the last three depend on `R : RankModel D` and `hK : R.ideal = KIdeal`. `stagePrototypes_finite` records finiteness of each stage.
